<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freiberg Mining Academy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as TU Bergakademie Freiberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a> Mining Academy (Bergakademie Freiberg) was founded in 1765 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-mining town of Freiberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, as part of the reconstruction after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, and is the oldest university of mining and metallurgy still in operation. Abraham Gottlob Werner taught geology there, and its students included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> and Novalis. Its chemists discovered the elements indium and germanium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/freiberg-mining-academy.jpg" alt="Freiberg Mining Academy" width="320" height="113" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bergakademie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TUBAF_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
